#
# ★★★ RESULT: CONCURRENT DUAL-BAND WORKS. Both radios beacon simultaneously,
# verified by an external scan from a separate host adapter:
#   BSS e0:1c:fc:51:c9:f0  freq 5180.0  SSID DIR842-OpenWrt   <- mainline rtw88
#   BSS 00:e0:4c:81:86:86  freq 2412.0  SSID DIR842-2G        <- this driver
# Client associated on 2.4 GHz with WPA2-PSK (4-way handshake IN-DRIVER, no
# hostapd), DHCP lease over the air, ping 8/8 0% loss @3.9ms, HT MCS4 43.3 Mbit/s.
# PCI 01:00.0 remains bound to rtw_8822be throughout.
#
# ★ THE BUG THAT KEPT IT SILENT: WRONG CRYSTAL (40 MHz vs 25 MHz).
# 8192cd_hw.c:14672 picks the WLAN crystal solely from CONFIG_PHY_EAT_40MHZ; the
# vendor's strap-reading alternative just above is dead code here (gated on
# CONFIG_AUTO_PCIE_PHY_SCAN, defined only for 8196E/__OSK__). With the flag set,
# XTAL_CLK_SEL_40M went into InitPONHandler() and the PLL came up for a 40 MHz part.
# LO off by 40/25 = 1.6x -> MAC/BB/firmware all report success, IQK completes,
# and NOTHING is on air; RX equally deaf (rx_packets 0). Three independent sources
# say 25 MHz: stock's own boot log on this unit, our mainline rtl8197f-wmac stub
# reading bootstrap SR+0x08 BIT(24), and the radio going loud when changed.
#
# ★ SECOND SILENT FAILURE: CONFIG_RTL_COMAPI_CFGFILE was NEVER set, so CfgFileProc()
# and its call site were both preprocessed away — the netifd handler that writes
# /etc/Wireless/RTL8192CD.dat would have been a silent no-op. Also CfgFileRead()
# called fp->f_op->read directly, which is NULL on tmpfs; now uses kernel_read().
